      "narrative": "The copper lamps hiss softly against the morning rain as you approach Mara Venn. She's leaning against the bar, one hand wrapped around a clay cup, her charcoal coat damp at the shoulders. Her eyes track you the moment you turn \u2014 she was already watching.\n\nYou clear your throat and ask about the Ash Gate.\n\nMara's expression doesn't shift. She takes a slow drink, sets the cup down, and says, \"You want to know about the Gate.\"\n\nShe waits, letting the silence stretch \u2014 testing whether you'll fill it with something foolish.\n\nThen she speaks, her voice low and flat. \"Old Bram worked that ferry for twenty-three years. Took the lockwright's seal, rowed folks through the mist, came back. Every time. Then one morning the Grey Hind's still tied to the dock, Bram's not in it, and the Gate's sealed from the inside. Toran \u2014\" she tilts her head toward the clerk at a nearby table, ink-stained and hunched over a ledger, \"\u2014 he's the one who locked it. Kestrel Guild's orders. Nobody through, nobody asking questions.\"\n\nShe watches you, waiting for you to catch up. \"That's what I know. That's all anyone in this room will tell you straight.\"\n\nHer eyes narrow a fraction. \"Unless you've got something sharper to ask than what the weather's like at the Gate.\"\n\nShe's testing you now \u2014 measuring whether you're worth more than idle curiosity."
    },
