      "narrative": "The moment the question leaves your mouth, something shifts in Mara's face. Not surprise \u2014 recognition. Like you've finally found the right door.\n\nShe sets her cup down with a soft clack and leans forward, forearms on the bar. Her voice drops to barely above a murmur.\n\n\"Bram.\" She says the name like it tastes old. \"Old Bram. He didn't just row the ferry \u2014 he ran the whole crossing. Sold the passage, collected the toll, knew every soul who went through and every soul who came back.\" She pauses. \"The Kestrel Guild owned the Gate on paper. But Bram owned the *business* of it. He decided who crossed and who didn't.\"\n\nShe glances toward the shuttered window \u2014 toward the direction of the Gate \u2014 then back at you.\n\n\"Twenty-three years, Kael. No complaints, no incidents, no bodies floating back through the mist. Then one morning he's dead in his boat, the Gate's sealed from the inside, and the lockwright's filing the key shut on guild orders before the sun's fully up.\"\n\nShe holds your gaze. \"Bram's the only one who knew who came through that last night. And Bram's not talking.\"\n\nA beat of silence. The rain drums against the shutters.\n\nMara's mouth tightens. \"But here's the piece that doesn't sit right with me \u2014 Bram was a ferryman. He collected coin, he rowed. He wasn't a fighter. Whoever killed him did it quiet, in the mist, and left him in his own boat with the door locked behind them.\" She taps a finger on the bar. \"That's not a robbery. That's a *silencing*.\"\n\nShe watches you. \"So tell me, ranger \u2014 does that sound like a question that ends at a dead man? Or does it sound like a thread worth pulling?\""
    },
