      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch groans shut behind you, cutting off the hiss of rain against copper lamps. The morning air hits you \u2014 damp and cold, carrying the smell of wet stone and old wood. The street is empty. Shuttered windows stare down like closed eyes.\n\nThe Ash Gate stands fifty paces ahead.\n\nIt's not a gate in the way you might have expected. No iron bars, no portcullis. It's a crack \u2014 a vertical wound in the grey stone of the city's eastern wall, sealed by a heavy disc of black iron as wide as a man's outstretched arms. The iron is bolted into the stone with rivets the size of your fist, their heads blistered with rust. A single keyhole gapes at its center, dark and empty.\n\nThe ferry dock juts out beside it \u2014 a short wooden pier where a small boat, the *Grey Hind*, sits tied to a cleat. The boat rocks gently on black water. Empty. Its wood is water-dark, its oars shipped neatly.\n\nThe mist beyond the Gate coils and shifts, thick as curdled milk, swallowing the far shore entirely.\n\nNo one else is here. The street behind you is silent. Only the lapping of water against the dock, and the faint drip of rain from the stone arch above the iron seal.\n\nThe keyhole stares at you. The boat waits. The mist does not stir."
    },
