      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch beneath the stone arch, fingers brushing the packed earth. The ground is dry here \u2014 sheltered from the rain by the overhang. Loose dirt, a scatter of brown leaves curled at the edges, a few small pebbles. Nothing disturbed. No scuff marks, no drag patterns, no impressions that don't belong to wind and weather.\n\nYou trail your fingers through the dirt, sweeping a slow arc. The soil shifts easily, undisturbed down to the hardpan beneath. If something was buried here, hidden here, dropped here \u2014 the ground gives no sign. Time and rain and wind have smoothed everything flat.\n\nThe iron seal looms above you. The keyhole remains empty. The *Grey Hind* knocks softly against the dock.\n\nFrom behind you, back toward the street, a voice \u2014 flat, unsurprised:\n\n\"Won't find anything there. I already looked.\"\n\nSable Quill stands at the corner of the nearest building, arms folded, travel-stained cloak hanging limp in the damp air. She watches you with the tired patience of someone who's been through this same exercise herself.\n\n\"I searched every inch of that arch three days ago. Ground, wall, the seal itself.\" She nods toward the iron disc. \"There's nothing hidden. The only thing that opens that gate is a key, and the only man who had it is dead.\""
