      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door swings open at your push. The warmth hits you first \u2014 the low fire, the smell of old ale and wet wool. A few heads turn and look away. The tavern is quieter than when you left. The morning crowd has thinned.\n\nMara Venn is behind the bar, wiping a cup with a rag. She looks up as you cross the floor, tracks you to the scarred table near the shuttered window \u2014 the same one you sat at before \u2014 and says nothing. Just watches.\n\nYour table is empty. The chair scrapes against the floorboards as you sit.\n\nThe fire pops. Somewhere in the back, a kettle begins to whistle.\n\nMara sets down the cup and walks over, rag slung over her shoulder. She stops at your table, looking down at you with that same sharp-eyed stillness.\n\n\"Back already.\" Not a question. \"Either you found nothing, or you found something you don't know what to do with.\"\n\nShe waits."
